  <p class="article-answer"><strong>The short answer:</strong> choose a registered landscape architect when the work requires landscape-architecture services, public-health and safety expertise, formal site planning or documents that must be prepared or sealed by that profession. Choose an experienced landscape designer or design-build landscape contractor for many residential garden, planting and outdoor-living projects—after confirming the exact scope, credentials and limits.</p>

  <h2 id="difference">The core difference is licensure</h2>
  <p>North Carolina law protects the title and practice of <em>landscape architect</em>. The <a href="https://www.ncleg.gov/Laws/GeneralStatuteSections/Chapter89A">North Carolina General Assembly’s Chapter 89A</a> defines the profession and prohibits unregistered people from using the designation or practicing landscape architecture.</p>
  <p>“Landscape designer” is broader. It may describe someone with horticultural, design, construction or practical garden experience, but the title itself does not tell you what training, insurance or legal authority the person has. Ask for specifics.</p>
  <p>A <em>landscape contractor</em> is a separate regulated role. The <a href="https://nclclb.com/licensing-2/about/">NC Landscape Contractors’ Licensing Board</a> administers the license for compensated landscape construction and contracting, including installation, planting, finish grading and other decorative utilitarian landscape work within the law’s scope.</p>

  <h2 id="roles">How the three roles compare</h2>
  <div class="article-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Typical strengths</th><th>Verify before hiring</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Landscape architect</td><td>Site planning, grading and drainage design, public or complex projects, technical documentation and health-safety-welfare responsibilities.</td><td>Active NC registration, relevant project experience and whether engineering or other consultants are also needed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Landscape designer</td><td>Residential layout, planting design, garden character, materials and collaboration with installers.</td><td>Education and experience, portfolio, deliverables, insurance and the boundary between design advice and regulated practice.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Licensed landscape contractor</td><td>Pricing and building landscape work, planting, garden construction, finish grading and coordinating field decisions.</td><td>Current license, business license where applicable, bond/insurance, project fit and whether permits, engineering or other licenses are required.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 id="choose">Who fits a residential project?</h2>
  <h3>A garden renovation or planting plan</h3><p>A horticulturally strong designer or design-build contractor may be a direct fit, especially when plant selection, seasonal structure and installation continuity are central.</p>
  <h3>A patio, path or residential outdoor room</h3><p>A landscape designer and licensed landscape contractor can often plan and build this work. Site conditions, project value, structures, electrical work, permits and engineering can add other required professionals.</p>
  <h3>A tall retaining wall, major grading or complex stormwater work</h3><p>Expect a more technical team. The project may need a registered landscape architect, professional engineer, surveyor or other licensed professional depending on the work and jurisdiction.</p>
  <h3>A public, commercial or approval-intensive site plan</h3><p>A landscape architect is often the appropriate lead or a required member of the team. Confirm requirements with the reviewing authority before design begins.</p>

  <aside class="article-note"><strong>Titles do not replace scope review</strong><p>No single credential makes every professional right for every project. Hire for the actual work, confirm who is responsible for each technical decision and ask when another discipline should be involved.</p></aside>

  <h2 id="questions">Questions that reveal the right fit</h2>
  <ol><li>What similar residential projects have you completed?</li><li>What will I receive: a concept, planting plan, construction details, estimate or full installation?</li><li>Which licenses do you and the business hold, and can I verify them?</li><li>Who is responsible for grading, drainage, structural details, permits and utility coordination?</li><li>What falls outside your scope?</li><li>If design and installation are separate, how will field questions be resolved?</li></ol>

  <h2 id="garden-art">Where Garden & Art fits</h2>
  <p>Garden & Art is led by Norman Rabins, a horticulturist and NC licensed landscape contractor (#1901). The practice focuses on residential landscape design and installation: gardens, planting, patios, paths, landscape walls, water management and related outdoor spaces within the appropriate scope.</p>
  <p>Garden & Art does not present Norman as a registered landscape architect. When a project needs engineering, landscape-architect services, foundation work or another regulated discipline, the responsible approach is to define that boundary and bring in the appropriate professional.</p>

